#: src/ai/text-threads.md:215
msgid "Advanced Concepts"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:217
msgid "Rule Templates"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:219
msgid ""
"Zed uses rule templates to power internal assistant features, like the "
"terminal assistant, or the content rules used in the inline assistant."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:221
msgid "Zed has the following internal rule templates:"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:223
msgid "`content_prompt.hbs`: Used for generating content in the editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:224
msgid ""
"`terminal_assistant_prompt.hbs`: Used for the terminal assistant feature."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:226
msgid ""
"At this point it is unknown if we will expand templates further to be user-"
"creatable."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:228
msgid "Overriding Templates"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:230
msgid ""
"**Note:** It is not recommended to override templates unless you know what "
"you are doing. Editing templates will break your assistant if done "
"incorrectly."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:232
msgid ""
"Zed allows you to override the default rules used for various assistant "
"features by placing custom Handlebars (.hbs) templates in your `~/.config/"
"zed/prompt_overrides` directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:234
msgid "The following templates can be overridden:"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:236
msgid ""
"[`content_prompt.hbs`](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/main/"
"assets/prompts/content_prompt.hbs): Used for generating content in the "
"editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:238
msgid ""
"[`terminal_assistant_prompt.hbs`](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/"
"main/assets/prompts/terminal_assistant_prompt.hbs): Used for the terminal "
"assistant feature."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:240
msgid ""
"**Note:** Be sure you want to override these, as you'll miss out on "
"iteration on our built-in features. This should be primarily used when "
"developing Zed."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:243
msgid ""
"You can customize these templates to better suit your needs while "
"maintaining the core structure and variables used by Zed. Zed will "
"automatically reload your prompt overrides when they change on disk."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:246
msgid ""
"Consult Zed's [assets/prompts](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/"
"main/assets/prompts) directory for current versions you can play with."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:248
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:250
msgid ""
"After you submit your first message in a text thread, a name for your "
"context is generated by the language model, and the context is automatically "
"saved to your file system in"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:252
msgid "`~/.config/zed/conversations` (macOS)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:253
msgid "`~/.local/share/zed/conversations` (Linux)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:254
msgid "`%LocalAppData%\\Zed\\conversations` (Windows)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:256
msgid ""
"You can access and load previous contexts by clicking on the history button "
"in the top-left corner of the agent panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/ai/text-threads.md:258
msgid ""
"![Viewing assistant history](https://zed.dev/img/assistant/assistant-history."
"png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:3
msgid "Collaboration"
msgstr "協作"

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:5
msgid ""
"Zed supports real-time multiplayer editing. Multiple people can work in the "
"same project simultaneously, seeing each other's cursors and edits as they "
"happen."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:7
msgid ""
"Open the Collaboration Panel with <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-shift-c\\|"
"ctrl-shift-c</kbd>. You'll need to [sign in](../authentication.md#signing-"
"in) to access collaboration features."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:9
msgid "Collaboration Panel"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:11
msgid "The Collaboration Panel has two sections:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:13
msgid ""
"[Channels](./channels.md): Persistent project rooms for team collaboration, "
"with shared projects and voice chat."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:14
msgid ""
"[Contacts and Private Calls](./contacts-and-private-calls.md): Your contacts "
"list for ad-hoc private sessions."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:16
msgid ""
"**Warning:** Sharing a project gives collaborators access to your local file "
"system within that project. Only collaborate with people you trust."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:18
msgid ""
"See the [Data and Privacy FAQs](https://zed.dev/faq#data-and-privacy) for "
"more details."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:20
msgid "Audio Settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:22
msgid "Selecting Audio Devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:24
msgid ""
"You can select specific input and output audio devices instead of using "
"system defaults. To configure audio devices:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:26
msgid "Open <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-,\\|ctrl-,</kbd>"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:27
msgid "Navigate to **Collaboration** > **Experimental**"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:28
msgid ""
"Use the **Output Audio Device** and **Input Audio Device** dropdowns to "
"select your preferred devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:30
msgid ""
"Changes take effect immediately. If you select a device that becomes "
"unavailable, Zed falls back to system defaults."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:32
msgid ""
"To test your audio configuration, click **Test Audio** in the same section. "
"This opens a window where you can verify your microphone and speaker work "
"correctly with the selected devices."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:34
msgid "**JSON configuration:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:38
msgid "\"audio\""
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:39
msgid "\"experimental.output_audio_device\""
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:39 src/collaboration/overview.md:40
msgid "\"Device Name (device-id)\""
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:40
msgid "\"experimental.input_audio_device\""
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:45
msgid "Set either value to `null` to use system defaults."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:3
msgid "Channels"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:5
msgid ""
"Channels are persistent rooms for team collaboration. Each channel can "
"contain shared projects, voice chat, and collaborative notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:7
msgid "Channels support:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:9
msgid ""
"Pairing – each collaborator keeps their own screen, mouse, and keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:10
msgid ""
"Mentoring – jump into someone else's context and help without asking them to "
"hand over control."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:11
msgid ""
"Refactoring – multiple people can join the same large refactor in real time."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:12
msgid ""
"Ambient awareness – see what teammates are working on without status "
"meetings."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:14
msgid ""
"Each channel usually maps to an ongoing project or workstream. You can see "
"who's in a channel because their avatars appear in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:17
msgid ""
"Create a channel by clicking the `+` icon next to the `Channels` text in the "
"Collaboration Panel. Create a subchannel by right-clicking an existing "
"channel and selecting `New Subchannel`."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:20
msgid ""
"You can keep both work and side-project channels in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:22
msgid ""
"Joining a channel adds you to a shared room where you can work on projects "
"together."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:24
msgid ""
"_Join [our channel tree](https://zed.dev/channel/zed-283) to get an idea of "
"how you can organize yours._"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:26
msgid "Inviting People"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:28
msgid ""
"By default, channels you create can only be accessed by you. You can invite "
"collaborators by right-clicking and selecting `Manage members`."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:31
msgid ""
"When you have subchannels nested under others, permissions are inherited. "
"For instance, adding people to the top-level channel in your channel tree "
"will automatically give them access to its subchannels."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:34
msgid ""
"Once you have added someone, they can either join your channel by clicking "
"on it in their Collaboration Panel, or you can share the link to the channel "
"so that they can join directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:36
msgid "Voice Chat"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:38
msgid ""
"You can mute/unmute your microphone via the microphone icon in the upper "
"right-hand side of the window."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:40
msgid ""
"**Note:** When joining a channel, Zed automatically shares your microphone "
"with other users in the call, if your OS allows it. To start muted, use the "
"[`mute_on_join`](../reference/all-settings.md#calls) setting."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:42
msgid "Sharing Projects"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:44
msgid ""
"After joining a channel, you can share a project over the channel via the "
"`Share` button in the upper right-hand side of the window. This will allow "
"channel members to edit the code hosted on your machine as though they had "
"it checked out locally."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:47
msgid ""
"When you edit someone else's project, your editor features still work: jump "
"to definition, use AI features, and view diagnostics. For pairing, one "
"person can implement while the other reads and validates nearby code. "
"Because you keep your own local configuration, the session still feels like "
"your normal setup."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:51
msgid ""
"Collaborators can open, edit, and save files, perform searches, and interact "
"with language servers. Guests have a read-only view of the project, "
"including access to language server info."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:54
msgid "Unsharing a Project"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:56
msgid ""
"You can remove a project from a channel by clicking on the `Unshare` button "
"in the title bar."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:58
msgid ""
"Collaborators that are currently in that project will be disconnected from "
"the project and will not be able to rejoin it unless you share it again."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:60
msgid "Channel Notes"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:62
msgid ""
"Each channel has a Markdown notes file associated with it to keep track of "
"current status, new ideas, or to collaborate on building out the design for "
"the feature that you're working on before diving into code."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:64
msgid ""
"This works like a shared Markdown document backed by Zed's collaboration "
"service."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:66
msgid ""
"Open channel notes by clicking the document icon to the right of the channel "
"name in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:68
msgid "**Note:** You can view a channel's notes without joining the channel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:70
msgid "Following Collaborators"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:72
msgid ""
"To follow a collaborator, click on their avatar in the top left of the title "
"bar. You can also cycle through collaborators using <kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">ctrl-alt-cmd-f\\|ctrl-alt-shift-f</kbd> or `workspace: "
"follow next collaborator` in the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:75
msgid ""
"When you join a project, you'll immediately start following the collaborator "
"that invited you."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:77
msgid "When a pane is following a collaborator, it will:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:79
msgid "follow their cursor and scroll position"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:80
msgid "follow them to other files in the same project"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:81
msgid ""
"instantly swap to viewing their screenshare in that pane, if they are "
"sharing their screen and leave the project"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:83
msgid ""
"To stop following, simply move your mouse or make an edit via your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:85
msgid "How Following Works"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:87
msgid ""
"Following is confined to a particular pane. When a pane is following a "
"collaborator, it is outlined in their cursor color."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:90
msgid ""
"Collaborators in the same project appear in color and include a cursor "
"color. Collaborators in other projects are shown in gray."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:93
msgid ""
"This pane-specific behavior allows you to follow someone in one pane while "
"navigating independently in another and can be an effective layout for some "
"collaboration styles."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:95
msgid "Following a Terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:97
msgid ""
"Following in terminals is not currently supported the same way it is in the "
"editor. As a workaround, collaborators can share their screen and you can "
"follow that instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:100
msgid "Screen Sharing"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:102
msgid ""
"Share your screen with collaborators in the current channel by clicking on "
"the `Share screen` (monitor icon) button in the top right of the title bar. "
"If you have multiple displays, you can choose which one to share via the "
"chevron to the right of the monitor icon."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:105
msgid ""
"After you've shared your screen, others can click the `Screen` entry under "
"your name in the Collaboration Panel to open a tab that keeps it visible. If "
"they are following you, Zed will automatically switch between following your "
"cursor in their Zed instance and your screen share, depending on whether you "
"are focused on Zed or another application, like a web browser."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:108
msgid ""
"**Warning:** Collaborators can see your entire screen when sharing. Stop "
"screen sharing when finished."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:110
msgid "Livestreaming & Guests"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:112
msgid ""
"A channel can also be made public. This allows anyone to join the channel by "
"clicking on the link."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:115
msgid ""
"Guest users in channels can hear and see everything that is happening, and "
"have read-only access to projects and channel notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:117
msgid ""
"If you'd like to invite a guest to participate in a channel for the duration "
"of a call, you can do so by right-clicking them in the Collaboration Panel. "
"\"Allowing Write Access\" will allow them to edit any projects shared into "
"the call, and to use their microphone and share their screen if they wish."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:120
msgid "Leaving a Call"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:122
msgid ""
"You can leave a channel by clicking on the `Leave call` button in the upper "
"right-hand side of the window."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:3
msgid "Contacts and Private Calls"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:5
msgid ""
"Private calls provide ad-hoc collaboration sessions outside of channels. Add "
"contacts to your list and start calls with one or more people."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:7
msgid "Adding a Contact"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:9
msgid ""
"In the Collaboration Panel, click the `+` button next to the `Contacts` "
"section"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:10
msgid ""
"Search for the contact using their GitHub handle.  \n"
"_Note: Your contact must be an existing Zed user who has completed the "
"GitHub authentication sign-in flow._"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:12
msgid ""
"Your contact will receive a notification. Once they accept, you'll both "
"appear in each other's contact list."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:15
msgid "Private Calls"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:17
msgid "To start a private call:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:19
msgid ""
"Click the `...` menu next to an online contact's name in the Collaboration "
"Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:20
msgid "Click `Call <username>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:22
msgid ""
"Once you've begun a private call, you can add other online contacts by "
"clicking their name in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:26
msgid ""
"_Private calls work like [channels](./channels.md), without channel-specific "
"features such as channel notes._"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/intellij.md:3
