#: src/toolchains.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed projects offer a dedicated UI for toolchain selection, which lets you "
"pick a set of tools for working with a given language in a current project."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:5
msgid ""
"Imagine you're working with Python project, which has virtual environments "
"that encapsulate a set of dependencies of your project along with a suitable "
"interpreter to run it with. The language server has to know which virtual "
"environment you're working with, as it uses it to understand your project's "
"code. With toolchain selector, you don't need to spend time configuring your "
"language server to point it at the right virtual environment directory—you "
"can just select the right virtual environment (toolchain) from a dropdown."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:8
msgid ""
"You can even select different toolchains for different subprojects within "
"your Zed project. A definition of a subproject is language-specific. In "
"collaborative scenarios, only the project owner can see and modify an active "
"toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:11
msgid ""
"In [remote projects](./remote-development.md), you can use the toolchain "
"selector to control the active toolchain on the SSH host. When [sharing your "
"project](./collaboration/overview.md), the toolchain selector is not "
"available to guests."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:13
msgid "Why do we need toolchains?"
msgstr "我們為什麼需要工具鏈？"

#: src/toolchains.md:15
msgid ""
"The active toolchain is relevant for launching language servers, which may "
"need it to function properly—it may not be able to resolve dependencies, "
"which in turn may make functionalities like \"Go to definition\" or \"Code "
"completions\" unavailable."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:17
msgid ""
"The active toolchain is also relevant when launching a shell in the terminal "
"panel: some toolchains provide \"activation scripts\" for shells, which make "
"those toolchains available in the shell environment for your convenience. "
"Zed will run these activation scripts automatically when you create a new "
"terminal."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:19
msgid ""
"This also applies to [tasks](./tasks.md)—Zed tasks behave \"as if\" you "
"opened a new terminal tab and ran a given task invocation yourself, which in "
"turn means that Zed task execution is affected by the active toolchain and "
"its activation script."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:21
msgid "Selecting toolchains"
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:23
msgid ""
"The active toolchain (if there is one) is displayed in the status bar (on "
"the right hand side). Click on it to access the toolchain selector—you can "
"also use an action from a command palette (<code class=\"hljs\">toolchain: "
"select</code>)."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:25
msgid ""
"Zed will automatically infer a set of toolchains to choose from based on the "
"project you're working with. A default will also be selected on your behalf "
"on a best-effort basis when you open a project for the first time."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:27
msgid ""
"The toolchain selection applies to a current subproject, which—depending on "
"the structure of your Zed project—might be your whole project or just a "
"subset of it. For example, if you have a monorepo with multiple subprojects, "
"you might want to select a different toolchain for each subproject."
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:29
msgid "Adding toolchains manually"
msgstr ""

#: src/toolchains.md:31
msgid ""
"If automatic detection does not suffice for you, you can add toolchains "
"manually. To do that, click on the \"Add toolchain\" button in the toolchain "
"selector. From there you can provide a path to a toolchain and set a name of "
"your liking for it."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:1
msgid "Configuring Zed"
msgstr "組態 Zed"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed is designed to be configured: we want to fit your workflow and "
"preferences exactly. We provide default settings that are designed to be a "
"comfortable starting point for as many people as possible, but we hope you "
"will enjoy tweaking it to make it feel incredible."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:5
msgid ""
"In addition to the settings described here, you may also want to change your "
"[theme](./themes.md), configure your [key bindings](./key-bindings.md), set "
"up [tasks](./tasks.md) or install [extensions](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/extensions)."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:7
msgid "Settings Editor"
msgstr "設定編輯器"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:9
msgid ""
"You can browse through many of the supported settings via the Settings "
"Editor, which can be opened with the <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-,|ctrl-,</"
"kbd> keybinding, or through the `zed: open settings` action in the command "
"palette. Through it, you can customize your local, user settings as well as "
"project settings."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:11
msgid ""
"Note that not all settings that Zed supports are available through the "
"Settings Editor yet. Some more intricate ones, such as language formatters, "
"can only be changed through the JSON settings file <kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">cmd-alt-,|ctrl-alt-,</kbd>."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:14
msgid "User Settings File"
msgstr "使用者設定檔"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:23
msgid ""
"Your settings JSON file can be opened with <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-"
"alt-,|ctrl-alt-,</kbd>. By default it is located at `~/.config/zed/settings."
"json`, though if you have `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in your environment on Linux it "
"will be at `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zed/settings.json` instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:26
msgid ""
"Whatever you have added to your user settings file gets merged with any "
"local configuration inside your projects."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:28
msgid "Default Settings"
msgstr "預設設定"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:30
msgid ""
"In the Settings Editor, the values you see set are the default ones. You can "
"also verify them in JSON by running <code class=\"hljs\">zed: open default "
"settings</code> from the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:33
msgid ""
"Extensions that provide language servers may also provide default settings "
"for those language servers."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:35
msgid "Project Settings File"
msgstr "專案設定檔"

#: src/configuring-zed.md:37
msgid ""
"Similarly to user files, you can open your project settings file by running "
"<code class=\"hljs\">zed: open project settings</code> from the command "
"palette. This will create a `.zed` directory containing`.zed/settings.json`."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:40
msgid ""
"Although most projects will only need one settings file at the root, you can "
"add more local settings files for subdirectories as needed. Not all settings "
"can be set in local files, just those that impact the behavior of the editor "
"and language tooling. For example you can set `tab_size`, `formatter` etc. "
"but not `theme`, `vim_mode` and similar."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:44
msgid ""
"The syntax for configuration files is a super-set of JSON that allows `//` "
"comments."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:46
msgid "Per-release Channel Overrides"
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:48
msgid ""
"Zed reads the same `settings.json` across all release channels (Stable, "
"Preview or Nightly). However, you can scope overrides to a specific channel "
"by adding top-level `stable`, `preview`, `nightly` or `dev` objects. They "
"are merged into the base configuration with settings from these keys taking "
"precedence upon launching the specified build. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:54
msgid "\"sunset\""
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:55 src/configuring-zed.md:58
#: src/configuring-zed.md:5076
msgid "\"vim_mode\""
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:56
msgid "\"nightly\""
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:57 src/configuring-zed.md:5066
msgid "\"cave-light\""
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:60
msgid "\"preview\""
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:61
msgid "\"zed-dark\""
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:66
msgid ""
"With this configuration, Stable keeps all base preferences, Preview switches "
"to `zed-dark`, and Nightly enables Vim mode with a different theme."
msgstr ""

#: src/configuring-zed.md:68
msgid ""
"Changing settings in the Settings Editorwill always apply the change across "
"all channels."
msgstr ""

