#: src/remote-development.md:178
msgid ""
"The project settings (in `.zed/settings.json` or `.editorconfig` of your "
"project)"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:180
msgid ""
"Both the local Zed and the server Zed read the project settings, but they "
"are not aware of the other's main settings file."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:182
msgid ""
"Which settings file you should use depends on the kind of setting you want "
"to make:"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:184
msgid ""
"Project settings should be used for things that affect the project: "
"indentation settings, which formatter / language server to use, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:185
msgid ""
"Server settings should be used for things that affect the server: paths to "
"language servers, proxy settings, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:186
msgid ""
"Local settings should be used for things that affect the UI: font size, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:188
msgid ""
"In addition any extensions you have installed locally will be propagated to "
"the remote server. This means that language servers, etc. will run correctly."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:190
msgid "Proxy Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:192
msgid ""
"The remote server will not use your local machine's proxy configuration "
"because they may be under different network policies. If your remote server "
"requires a proxy to access the internet, you must configure it on the remote "
"server itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:194
msgid ""
"In most cases, your remote server will already have proxy environment "
"variables configured. Zed will automatically use them when downloading "
"language servers, communicating with LLM models, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:196
msgid ""
"If needed, you can set these environment variables in the server's shell "
"configuration (e.g., `~/.bashrc`):"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:199 src/remote-development.md:200
#: src/remote-development.md:208
msgid "\"http://proxy.example.com:8080\""
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:201
msgid "\"localhost,127.0.0.1\""
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:204
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can configure the proxy in the remote machine's `~/."
"config/zed/settings.json` (Linux) or `~/.zed/settings.json` (macOS):"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:212
msgid ""
"See the [proxy documentation](./reference/all-settings.md#network-proxy) for "
"supported proxy types and additional configuration options."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:214
msgid "Initializing the remote server"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:216
msgid ""
"Once you provide the SSH options, Zed shells out to `ssh` on your local "
"machine to create a ControlMaster connection with the options you provide."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:218
msgid ""
"Any prompts that SSH needs will be shown in the UI, so you can verify host "
"keys, type key passwords, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:220
msgid ""
"Once the master connection is established, Zed will check to see if the "
"remote server binary is present in `~/.zed_server` on the remote, and that "
"its version matches the current version of Zed that you're using."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:222
msgid ""
"If it is not there or the version mismatches, Zed will try to download the "
"latest version. By default, it will download from `https://zed.dev` "
"directly, but if you set: `{\"upload_binary_over_ssh\":true}` in your "
"settings for that server, it will download the binary to your local machine "
"and then upload it to the remote server."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:224
msgid ""
"If you'd like to maintain the server binary yourself you can. You can either "
"download our prebuilt versions from [GitHub](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/zed/releases), or [build your own](https://zed.dev/docs/"
"development) with `cargo build -p remote_server --release`. If you do this, "
"you must upload it to `~/.zed_server/zed-remote-server-{RELEASE_CHANNEL}-"
"{VERSION}` on the server, for example `~/.zed_server/zed-remote-server-"
"stable-0.217.3+stable.105.80433cb239e868271457ac376673a5f75bc4adb1`. The "
"version must exactly match the version of Zed itself you are using."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:226
msgid "Maintaining the SSH connection"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:228
msgid ""
"Once the server is initialized. Zed will create new SSH connections (reusing "
"the existing ControlMaster) to run the remote development server."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:230
msgid ""
"Each connection tries to run the development server in proxy mode. This mode "
"will start the daemon if it is not running, and reconnect to it if it is. "
"This way when your connection drops and is restarted, you can continue to "
"work without interruption."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:232
msgid ""
"In the case that reconnecting fails, the daemon will not be re-used. That "
"said, unsaved changes are by default persisted locally, so that you do not "
"lose work. You can always reconnect to the project at a later date and Zed "
"will restore unsaved changes."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:234
msgid ""
"If you are struggling with connection issues, you should be able to see more "
"information in the Zed log `cmd-shift-p Open Log`. If you are seeing things "
"that are unexpected, please file a [GitHub issue](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/zed/issues/new) or reach out in the #support forums on [Discord]"
"(https://zed.dev/community-links)."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:236
msgid "Supported SSH Options"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:238
msgid ""
"Under the hood, Zed shells out to the `ssh` binary to connect to the remote "
"server. We create one SSH control master per project, and then use that to "
"multiplex SSH connections for the Zed protocol itself, any terminals you "
"open and tasks you run. We read settings from your SSH config file, but if "
"you want to specify additional options to the SSH control master you can "
"configure Zed to set them."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:240
msgid ""
"When typing in the \"Connect New Server\" dialog, you can use bash-style "
"quoting to pass options containing a space. Once you have created a server "
"it will be added to the `\"ssh_connections\": []` array in your settings "
"file. You can edit the settings file directly to make changes to SSH "
"connections."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:242
msgid "Supported options:"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:244
msgid ""
"`-p` / `-l` - these are equivalent to passing the port and the username in "
"the host string."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:245
msgid "`-L` / `-R` for port forwarding"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:246
msgid "`-i` - to use a specific key file"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:247
msgid "`-o` - to set custom options"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:248
msgid "`-J` / `-w` - to proxy the SSH connection"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:249
msgid "`-F` for specifying an `ssh_config`"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:250
msgid ""
"And also... `-4`, `-6`, `-A`, `-B`, `-C`, `-D`, `-I`, `-K`, `-P`, `-X`, `-"
"Y`, `-a`, `-b`, `-c`, `-i`, `-k`, `-l`, `-m`, `-o`, `-p`, `-w`, `-x`, `-y`"
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:252
msgid ""
"Note that we deliberately disallow some options (for example `-t` or `-T`) "
"that Zed will set for you."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:256
msgid ""
"You can't open files from the remote Terminal by typing the `zed` command."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:260
msgid ""
"[Running & Testing](./running-testing.md): Run tasks, terminal commands, and "
"debugger sessions while you work remotely."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:262
msgid ""
"[Configuring Zed](./configuring-zed.md): Manage shared and project settings, "
"including `.zed/settings.json`."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:264
msgid ""
"[Agent Panel](./ai/agent-panel.md): Use AI workflows in remote projects."
msgstr ""

