#: src/collaboration/overview.md:3
msgid ""
"At Zed, we believe that great things are built by great people working "
"together. We have designed Zed to help individuals work faster and help "
"teams of people work together more effectively."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:6
msgid ""
"In Zed, all collaboration happens in the collaboration panel, which can be "
"opened via <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-shift-c|ctrl-shift-c</kbd> or "
"`collab panel: toggle focus` from the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:8
msgid ""
"You will need to [sign in](../authentication.md#signing-in) in order to "
"access features within the collaboration panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:10
msgid "Collaboration panel"
msgstr "協作面板"

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:12
msgid "The collaboration panel is broken down into two sections:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:14
msgid ""
"[Channels](./channels.md): Ongoing project rooms where team members can "
"share projects, collaborate on code, and maintain ambient awareness of what "
"everyone is working on."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:15
msgid ""
"[Contacts and Private Calls](./contacts-and-private-calls.md): Your contacts "
"list for ad-hoc private collaboration."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:19
msgid ""
"Note: Only collaborate with people that you trust. Since sharing a project "
"gives them access to your local file system, you should not share projects "
"with people you do not trust; they could potentially do some nasty things."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:22
msgid ""
"In the future, we will do more to prevent this type of access beyond the "
"shared project and add more control over what collaborators can do, but for "
"now, only collaborate with people you trust."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/overview.md:24
msgid ""
"See our [Data and Privacy FAQs](https://zed.dev/faq#data-and-privacy) for "
"collaboration."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:3
msgid ""
"Channels provide a way to streamline collaborating for software engineers in "
"many ways, but particularly:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:5
msgid ""
"Pairing – when working on something together, you both have your own screen, "
"mouse, and keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:6
msgid ""
"Mentoring – it's easy to jump in to someone else's context, and help them "
"get unstuck, without the friction of pushing code up."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:7
msgid ""
"Refactoring – you can have multiple people join in on large refactoring "
"without fear of conflict."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:8
msgid ""
"Ambient awareness – you can see what everyone else is working on with no "
"need for status emails or meetings."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:10
msgid ""
"Each channel corresponds to an ongoing project or work-stream. You can see "
"who's in a channel as their avatars will show up in the sidebar. This makes "
"it easy to see what everyone is doing and where to find them if needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:14
msgid ""
"Create a channel by clicking the `+` icon next to the `Channels` text in the "
"collab panel. Create a subchannel by right clicking an existing channel and "
"selecting `New Subchannel`."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:17
msgid ""
"You can mix channels for your day job, as well as side-projects in your "
"collab panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:25
msgid ""
"By default, channels you create can only be accessed by you. You can invite "
"collaborators by right clicking and selecting `Manage members`."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:31
msgid ""
"Once you have added someone, they can either join your channel by clicking "
"on it in their Zed sidebar, or you can share the link to the channel so that "
"they can join directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:37
msgid ""
"Note: When joining a channel, Zed will automatically share your microphone "
"with other users in the call, if your OS allows it. If you'd prefer your "
"microphone to be off when joining a channel, you can do so via the "
"[`mute_on_join`](../configuring-zed.md#calls) setting."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:45
msgid ""
"When you are editing someone else's project, you still have the full power "
"of the editor at your fingertips; you can jump to definitions, use the AI "
"assistant, and see any diagnostic errors. This is extremely powerful for "
"pairing, as one of you can be implementing the current method while the "
"other is reading and researching the correct solution to the next problem. "
"And, because you have your own config running, it feels like you're using "
"your own machine."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:49
msgid ""
"We aim to eliminate the distinction between local and remote projects as "
"much as possible. Collaborators can open, edit, and save files, perform "
"searches, interact with the language server, etc. Guests have a read-only "
"view of the project, including access to language server info."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:63
msgid ""
"This is similar to a Google Doc, except powered by Zed's collaborative "
"software and persisted to our servers."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:65
msgid ""
"Open the channel notes by clicking on the document icon to the right of the "
"channel name in the collaboration panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:67
msgid ""
"Note: You can view a channel's notes without joining the channel, if you'd "
"just like to read up on what has been written."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:71
msgid ""
"To follow a collaborator, click on their avatar in the top left of the title "
"bar. You can also cycle through collaborators using <kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">ctrl-alt-cmd-f|ctrl-alt-shift-f</kbd> or `workspace: "
"follow next collaborator` in the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:76
msgid "When you are in a pane that is following a collaborator, you will:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:89
msgid ""
"Avatars of collaborators in the same project as you are in color, and have a "
"cursor color. Collaborators in other projects are shown in gray."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:96
msgid ""
"Following is not currently supported in the terminal in the way it is "
"supported in the editor. As a workaround, collaborators can share their "
"screen and you can follow that instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:104
msgid ""
"After you've shared your screen, others can click on the `Screen` entry "
"under your name in the collaboration panel to open a tab that always keeps "
"it visible. If they are following you, Zed will automatically switch between "
"following your cursor in their Zed instance and your screen share, depending "
"on whether you are focused on Zed or another application, like a web browser."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:107
msgid ""
"Note: Collaborators can see your entire screen when you are screen sharing, "
"so be careful not to share anything you don't want to share. Remember to "
"stop screen sharing when you are finished."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:112
msgid ""
"A Channel can also be made Public. This allows anyone to join the channel by "
"clicking on the link."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:115
msgid ""
"Guest users in channels can hear and see everything that is happening, and "
"have read only access to projects and channel notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:117
msgid ""
"If you'd like to invite a guest to participate in a channel for the duration "
"of a call you can do so by right clicking on them in the Collaboration "
"Panel. \"Allowing Write Access\" will allow them to edit any projects shared "
"into the call, and to use their microphone and share their screen if they "
"wish."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed allows you to have private calls / collaboration sessions with those in "
"your contacts. These calls can be one-on-ones or contain any number of users "
"from your contacts."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:8
msgid ""
"In the collaboration panel, click the `+` button next to the `Contacts` "
"section"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:16
msgid "To start up a private call..."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:18
msgid ""
"Click the `...` menu next to an online contact's name in the collaboration "
"panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:21
msgid ""
"Once you've begun a private call, you can add other online contacts by "
"clicking on their name in the collaboration panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/contacts-and-private-calls.md:25
msgid ""
"_Aside from a few additional features (channel notes, etc.), collaboration "
"in private calls is largely the same as it is in [channels](./channels.md)._"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:1
msgid "Command-line Interface"
msgstr "命令行介面"

#: src/command-line-interface.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed has a CLI, on Linux this should come with the distribution's Zed package "
"(binary name can vary from distribution to distribution, `zed` will be used "
"later for brevity). For macOS, the CLI comes in the same package with the "
"editor binary, and could be installed into the system with the `cli: "
"install` Zed command which will create a symlink to the `/usr/local/bin/"
"zed`. It can also be built from source out of the `cli` crate in this "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:7
msgid ""
"Use `zed --help` to see the full list of capabilities. General highlights:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:10
msgid "Opening another empty Zed window: `zed`"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:12
msgid ""
"Opening a file or directory in Zed: `zed /path/to/entry` (use `-n` to open "
"in the new window)"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:14
msgid "Reading from stdin: `ps axf | zed -`"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:16
msgid "Starting Zed with logs in the terminal: `zed --foreground`"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-interface.md:18
msgid "Uninstalling Zed and all its related files: `zed --uninstall`"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-palette.md:3
msgid ""
"The Command Palette is the main way to access pretty much any functionality "
"that's available in Zed. Its keybinding is the first one you should make "
"yourself familiar with. To open it, hit: <kbd class=\"keybinding\">cmd-shift-"
"p|ctrl-shift-p</kbd>."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-palette.md:5
msgid ""
"![The opened Command Palette](https://zed.dev/img/features/command-palette."
"jpg)"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-palette.md:7
msgid ""
"Try it! Open the Command Palette and type in `new file`. You should see the "
"list of commands being filtered down to `workspace: new file`. Hit return "
"and you end up with a new buffer."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-palette.md:9
msgid ""
"Any time you see instructions that include commands of the form `zed: ...` "
"or `editor: ...` and so on that means you need to execute them in the "
"Command Palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/debugger.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed uses the [Debug Adapter Protocol (DAP)](https://microsoft.github.io/"
"debug-adapter-protocol/) to provide debugging functionality across multiple "
"programming languages. DAP is a standardized protocol that defines how "
"debuggers, editors, and IDEs communicate with each other. It allows Zed to "
"support various debuggers without needing to implement language-specific "
"debugging logic. Zed implements the client side of the protocol, and various "
"_debug adapters_ implement the server side."
msgstr ""

#: src/debugger.md:8
msgid ""
"This protocol enables features like setting breakpoints, stepping through "
"code, inspecting variables, and more, in a consistent manner across "
"different programming languages and runtime environments."
msgstr ""

#: src/debugger.md:11
msgid "Supported Languages"
msgstr ""

#: src/debugger.md:13
msgid ""
"To debug code written in a specific language, Zed needs to find a debug "
"adapter for that language. Some debug adapters are provided by Zed without "
"additional setup, and some are provided by [language extensions](./"
"extensions/debugger-extensions.md). The following languages currently have "
"debug adapters available:"
msgstr ""

#: src/debugger.md:17
msgid "[C](./languages/c.md#debugging) (built-in)"
msgstr ""

