msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:11
msgid ""
"The outline panel truly excels when used with multi-buffers. Here are some "
"examples of its versatility:"
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:13
msgid "Project Search Results"
msgstr "專案搜尋結果"

#: src/outline-panel.md:15
msgid "Get an overview of search results across your project."
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:17
msgid ""
"![Using the outline panel in a project search multi-buffer](https://zed.dev/"
"img/outline-panel/project-search.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:19
msgid "Project Diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:21
msgid ""
"View a summary of all errors and warnings reported by the language server."
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:23
msgid ""
"![Using the outline panel while viewing project diagnostics multi-buffer]"
"(https://zed.dev/img/outline-panel/project-diagnostics.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:25
msgid "Find All References"
msgstr "尋找所有參考"

#: src/outline-panel.md:27
msgid ""
"Quickly navigate through all references when using the `editor: find all "
"references` action."
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:29
msgid ""
"![Using the outline panel while viewing `find all references` multi-buffer]"
"(https://zed.dev/img/outline-panel/find-all-references.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/outline-panel.md:31
msgid ""
"The outline view provides a great way to quickly navigate to specific parts "
"of your code and helps you maintain context when working with large result "
"sets in multi-buffers."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:3
msgid ""
"Remote Development allows you to code at the speed of thought, even when "
"your codebase is not on your local machine. You use Zed locally so the UI is "
"immediately responsive, but offload heavy computation to the development "
"server so that you can work effectively."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:11
msgid ""
"On your local machine, Zed runs its UI, talks to language models, uses Tree-"
"sitter to parse and syntax-highlight code, and store unsaved changes and "
"recent projects. The source code, language servers, tasks, and the terminal "
"all run on the remote server."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:18
msgid ""
"Use <kbd class=\"keybinding\">ctrl-cmd-shift-o|alt-ctrl-shift-o</kbd> to "
"open the \"Remote Projects\" dialog."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:36
msgid ""
"The list of remote servers is stored in your settings file <kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">cmd-,|ctrl-,</kbd>. You can edit this list using the "
"Remote Projects dialog <kbd class=\"keybinding\">ctrl-cmd-shift-o|alt-ctrl-"
"shift-o</kbd>, which provides some robustness - for example it checks that "
"the connection can be established before writing it to the settings file."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:175
msgid ""
"Both the local Zed and the server Zed read the project settings, but they "
"are not aware of the other's main `settings.json`."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:207
msgid ""
"See the [proxy documentation](./configuring-zed.md#network-proxy) for "
"supported proxy types and additional configuration options."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:229
msgid ""
"If you are struggling with connection issues, you should be able to see more "
"information in the Zed log `cmd-shift-p Open Log`. If you are seeing things "
"that are unexpected, please file a [GitHub issue](https://github.com/zed-"
"industries/zed/issues/new) or reach out in the #remoting-feedback channel in "
"the [Zed Discord](https://zed.dev/community-links)."
msgstr ""

#: src/remote-development.md:255
msgid ""
"Please join the #remoting-feedback channel in the [Zed Discord](https://zed."
"dev/community-links)."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:5
msgid ""
"Bring the power of [Jupyter kernels](https://docs.jupyter.org/en/latest/"
"projects/kernels.html) to your editor! The built-in REPL for Zed allows you "
"to run code interactively in your editor similarly to a notebook with your "
"own text files."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:26
msgid ""
"Zed supports running code in multiple languages. To get started, you need to "
"install a kernel for the language you want to use."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:28
msgid "**Currently supported languages:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:30
msgid "[Python (ipykernel)](#python)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:31
msgid "[TypeScript (Deno)](#typescript-deno)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:32
msgid "[R (Ark)](#r-ark)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:33
msgid "[R (Xeus)](#r-xeus)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:34
msgid "[Julia](#julia)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:35
msgid "[Scala (Almond)](#scala)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:37
msgid ""
"Once installed, you can start using the REPL in the respective language "
"files, or other places those languages are supported, such as Markdown. If "
"you recently added the kernels, run the `repl: refresh kernelspecs` command "
"to make them available in the editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:39
msgid "Using the REPL"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:41
msgid ""
"To start the REPL, open a file with the language you want to use and use the "
"`repl: run` command (defaults to `ctrl-shift-enter` on macOS) to run a "
"block, selection, or line. You can also click on the REPL icon in the "
"toolbar."
msgstr ""

