#: src/telemetry.md:3
msgid ""
"Zed collects anonymous telemetry data to help the team understand how people "
"are using the application and to see what sort of issues they are "
"experiencing."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:7
msgid ""
"You have full control over what data is sent out by Zed. To enable or "
"disable some or all telemetry types, open your `settings.json` file via "
"<code class=\"hljs\">zed: open settings</code>(<kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">cmd-,|ctrl-,</kbd>) from the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:10
msgid "Insert and tweak the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:21
msgid ""
"Telemetry is sent from the application to our servers. Data is proxied "
"through our servers to enable us to easily switch analytics services. We "
"currently use:"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:32
msgid ""
"Crash reports consist of a [minidump](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/"
"windows/win32/debug/minidump-files) and some extra debug information. "
"Reports are sent on the first application launch after the crash occurred. "
"We've built dashboards that allow us to visualize the frequency and severity "
"of issues experienced by users. Having these reports sent automatically "
"allows us to begin implementing fixes without the user needing to file a "
"report in our issue tracker. The plots in the dashboards also give us an "
"informal measurement of the stability of Zed."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:34
msgid ""
"You can see what extra data is sent alongside the minidump in the `Panic` "
"struct in [crates/telemetry_events/src/telemetry_events.rs](https://github."
"com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/telemetry_events/src/"
"telemetry_events.rs) in the Zed repo. You can find additional information in "
"the [Debugging Crashes](./development/debugging-crashes.md) documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:36
msgid "Client-Side Usage Data"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:38
msgid ""
"To improve Zed and understand how it is being used in the wild, Zed "
"optionally collects usage data like the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:40
msgid "(a) file extensions of opened files;"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:41
msgid "(b) features and tools You use within the Editor;"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:42
msgid "(c) project statistics (e.g., number of files); and"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:43
msgid "(d) frameworks detected in Your projects"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:45
msgid ""
"Usage Data does not include any of Your software code or sensitive project "
"details. Metric events are reported over HTTPS, and requests are rate-"
"limited to avoid using significant network bandwidth."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:47
msgid ""
"Usage Data is associated with a secure random telemetry ID which may be "
"linked to Your email address. This linkage currently serves two purposes: "
"(1) it allows Zed to analyze usage patterns over time while maintaining Your "
"privacy; and (2) it enables Zed to reach out to specific user groups for "
"feedback and improvement suggestions."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:49
msgid ""
"You can audit the metrics data that Zed has reported by running the command "
"<code class=\"hljs\">zed: open telemetry log</code> from the command "
"palette, or clicking `Help > View Telemetry Log` in the application menu."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:51
msgid ""
"You can see the full list of the event types and exactly the data sent for "
"each by inspecting the `Event` enum and the associated structs in [crates/"
"telemetry_events/src/telemetry_events.rs](https://github.com/zed-industries/"
"zed/blob/main/crates/telemetry_events/src/telemetry_events.rs) in the Zed "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:53
msgid "Server-Side Usage Data"
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:55
msgid ""
"When using Zed's hosted services, we may collect, generate, and Process data "
"to allow us to support users and improve our hosted offering. Examples "
"include metadata around rate limiting and billing metrics/token usage. Zed "
"does not persistently store user content or use user content to evaluate and/"
"or improve our AI features, unless it is explicitly shared with Zed, and we "
"have a zero-data retention agreement with Anthropic."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:57
msgid ""
"You can see more about our stance on data collection (and that any prompt "
"data shared with Zed is explicitly opt-in) at [AI Improvement](./ai/ai-"
"improvement.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/telemetry.md:61
msgid ""
"If you have concerns about telemetry, please feel free to [open an issue]"
"(https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/issues/new/choose)."
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:5
msgid ""
"This guide covers common troubleshooting techniques for Zed. Sometimes "
"you'll be able to identify and resolve issues on your own using this "
"information. Other times, troubleshooting means gathering the right "
"information (logs, profiles, or reproduction steps) to help us diagnose and "
"fix the problem."
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:9
msgid ""
"**Note**: To open the command palette, use `cmd-shift-p` on macOS or `ctrl-"
"shift-p` on Windows / Linux."
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:11
msgid "Retrieve Zed and System Information"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:13
msgid ""
"When reporting issues or seeking help, it's useful to know your Zed version "
"and system specifications. You can retrieve this information using the "
"following actions from the command palette:"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:15
msgid "<code class=\"hljs\">zed: about</code>: Find your Zed version number"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:16
msgid ""
"<code class=\"hljs\">zed: copy system specs into clipboard</code>: Populate "
"your clipboard with Zed version number, operating system version, and "
"hardware specs"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:17
msgid ""
"<code class=\"hljs\">zed: copy installed extensions into clipboard</code>: "
"Populate your clipboard with a list of your installed extensions and versions"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:19
msgid "Zed Log"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:21
msgid ""
"Often, a good first place to look when troubleshooting any issue in Zed is "
"the Zed log, which might contain clues about what's going wrong. You can "
"review the most recent 1000 lines of the log by running the <code "
"class=\"hljs\">zed: open log</code> action from the command palette. If you "
"want to view the full file, you can reveal it in your operating system's "
"native file manager via <code class=\"hljs\">zed: reveal log in file "
"manager</code> from the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:25
msgid ""
"You'll find the Zed log in the respective location on each operating system:"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:27
msgid "macOS: `~/Library/Logs/Zed/Zed.log`"
msgstr "macOS：`~/Library/Logs/Zed/Zed.log`"

#: src/troubleshooting.md:28
msgid "Windows: `C:\\Users\\YOU\\AppData\\Local\\Zed\\logs\\Zed.log`"
msgstr "Windows：`C:\\Users\\YOU\\AppData\\Local\\Zed\\logs\\Zed.log`"

#: src/troubleshooting.md:29
msgid "Linux: `~/.local/share/zed/logs/Zed.log` or `$XDG_DATA_HOME`"
msgstr "Linux：`~/.local/share/zed/logs/Zed.log` 或 `$XDG_DATA_HOME`"

#: src/troubleshooting.md:31
msgid ""
"**Note:** In some cases, it might be useful to monitor the log live, such as "
"when [developing a Zed extension](https://zed.dev/docs/extensions/developing-"
"extensions). Example: `tail -f ~/Library/Logs/Zed/Zed.log`"
msgstr ""

#: src/troubleshooting.md:34
